    {
      "id": 3,
      "kind": "F_retrieval",
      "payload": "{\"characters\":0,\"documents\":[],\"llm_tokens\":0,\"query\":\"A locality SIMD OpenMP\"}",
      "created": 1792128676.4304945
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "kind": "mother_usage",
      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"preparation\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Own the exact BFS ordering and frontier. The official tie-break is U, D, L, R, but the source generates R, L, U, D and prepends each successor to the next-level list, reversing FIFO discovery order. Investigate a stable FIFO frontier (for example, ordered current/next-level arrays) with moves enqueued U, D, L, R. Keep exact state identity and first-discovery parent links so the result remains shortest and lexicographically first. Measure the change; do not assume the four baseline cases exercise tied solutions.\",\"B\":\"Own per-state move-generation costs while retaining the same BFS semantics and exact state key. The source scans all boxes for occupancy on each move and bubble-sorts box positions after every transition. Investigate building an occupancy map once per expanded state, then updating the moved box in canonical sorted order without quadratic sorting. Precomputed neighbor indices or bounds checks may be evaluated separately. Preserve all legal moves and handle board edges safely; measure the complete candidate, not just the transition routine.\",\"C\":\"Explore a higher-risk, movement-optimal push-state search. Represent the initial state and states immediately after pushes; with boxes fixed, find shortest player walks to legal push stances, then generate push transitions weighted by walking distance plus one. Use Dijkstra, not unweighted push-count search, because the required answer minimizes player movements. Retain exact player position, enough predecessor/route information to reconstruct the path, and lexicographic tie handling across complete equal-length paths in U, D, L, R order. Keep this candidate only if it is proven to match shortest-path and tie-break requirements on valid inputs; optional deadlock pruning must be sound and measured separately.\",\"G\":\"Wait for independent A/B/C candidates. Check correctness and output ordering before integration; reject test-specific assumptions, unsafe pruning, and any change that substitutes expected answers. Measure each candidate and every integrated combination on the same target and prescribed workload. The baseline is valid on 4 cases with mean 0.0048162698 seconds and score 0.5, so any gain may be small or noisy; do not infer an integrated gain from individual results. Preserve the best fully measured correct variant, and do not change tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, permissions, or other protected inputs.\",\"analysis\":\"The measured baseline is correct on its four cases, with a 4.816 ms mean, but the source has no automatic profile identifying a dominant cost and the observed corpus is small. A notable semantic risk is that its move order and prepended next-level list do not implement the stated shortest-path tie-break. A addresses that while preserving BFS; B targets obvious transition overhead; C tests a distinct state-space reduction with movement costs and exact tie handling. The ARM64 target has four cores, but parallel search is not assumed beneficial and could complicate deterministic ordering. Judge every candidate and combination by correctness and end-to-end measurements; all valid inputs must remain supported.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"aa16077161df0c1f96731a0a6b4d5cf400e510501fa22c211f6ebbd9fa9ff979\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Own the exact BFS ordering and frontier. The official tie-break is U, D, L, R, but the source generates R, L, U, D and prepends each successor to the next-level list, reversing FIFO discovery order. Investigate a stable FIFO frontier (for example, ordered current/next-level arrays) with moves enqueued U, D, L, R. Keep exact state identity and first-discovery parent links so the result remains shortest and lexicographically first. Measure the change; do not assume the four baseline cases exercise tied solutions.\",\"B\":\"Own per-state move-generation costs while retaining the same BFS semantics and exact state key. The source scans all boxes for occupancy on each move and bubble-sorts box positions after every transition. Investigate building an occupancy map once per expanded state, then updating the moved box in canonical sorted order without quadratic sorting. Precomputed neighbor indices or bounds checks may be evaluated separately. Preserve all legal moves and handle board edges safely; measure the complete candidate, not just the transition routine.\",\"C\":\"Explore a higher-risk, movement-optimal push-state search. Represent the initial state and states immediately after pushes; with boxes fixed, find shortest player walks to legal push stances, then generate push transitions weighted by walking distance plus one. Use Dijkstra, not unweighted push-count search, because the required answer minimizes player movements. Retain exact player position, enough predecessor/route information to reconstruct the path, and lexicographic tie handling across complete equal-length paths in U, D, L, R order. Keep this candidate only if it is proven to match shortest-path and tie-break requirements on valid inputs; optional deadlock pruning must be sound and measured separately.\",\"G\":\"Wait for independent A/B/C candidates. Check correctness and output ordering before integration; reject test-specific assumptions, unsafe pruning, and any change that substitutes expected answers. Measure each candidate and every integrated combination on the same target and prescribed workload. The baseline is valid on 4 cases with mean 0.0048162698 seconds and score 0.5, so any gain may be small or noisy; do not infer an integrated gain from individual results. Preserve the best fully measured correct variant, and do not change tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, permissions, or other protected inputs.\",\"analysis\":\"The measured baseline is correct on its four cases, with a 4.816 ms mean, but the source has no automatic profile identifying a dominant cost and the observed corpus is small. A notable semantic risk is that its move order and prepended next-level list do not implement the stated shortest-path tie-break. A addresses that while preserving BFS; B targets obvious transition overhead; C tests a distinct state-space reduction with movement costs and exact tie handling. The ARM64 target has four cores, but parallel search is not assumed beneficial and could complicate deterministic ordering. Judge every candidate and combination by correctness and end-to-end measurements; all valid inputs must remain supported.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"nonce\":\"5ac7554709df4bfa8667466001b1f320\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"max\",\"request_sha256\":\"aa16077161df0c1f96731a0a6b4d5cf400e510501fa22c211f6ebbd9fa9ff979\",\"session_id\":\"c08531b8446a447bacb06c4500c0c033\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"d1017b653a0c88066202f205157de710b1f4866538528362ab6aa3592d10d630\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"cached_input_tokens\":0,\"input_tokens\":13571,\"output_tokens\":6613,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":5996}],\"llm_calls\":1,\"model_requested\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"reasoning_effort_requested\":\"max\",\"token_accounting\":\"exact\",\"tokens\":20184,\"tool_events\":[]}},\"status\":\"complete\"}",
